# API

| ID | Topic | Summary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `API-0001` | Scope | Treat every stable interaction boundary as an API contract. |
| `API-0002` | Ownership | Keep interface adapters from becoming accidental behavior authority. |
| `API-0003` | Identity | Expose durable identifiers rather than temporary locators. |
| `API-0004` | Contracts | Prefer explicit versionable request, response, event, and schema shapes. |


## API-0001 - Scope

An API may be HTTP, RPC, CLI, library call, message, file format, plugin contract, event, database integration boundary, or another stable interface. The contract is defined by observable behavior, not by transport technology.

## API-0002 - Ownership

Adapters should delegate stable project behavior to the layer that owns it. Transport parsing, serialization, authentication plumbing, and framework-specific request objects should remain local unless intentionally part of the public contract.

## API-0003 - Identity

Public identity should use documented stable identifiers. Session values, signed URLs, generated paths, transient handles, and short-lived provider tokens should not be presented as durable identity unless explicitly guaranteed by the contract.

## API-0004 - Contracts

Important boundaries should define required and optional fields, validation behavior, normalization, versioning, and compatibility expectations. Avoid unintentionally exposing framework, ORM, serializer, or vendor-SDK internals as the stable schema.
</file>

