# Architecture

| ID | Topic | Summary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ARCH-0001` | Ownership | Record what each major architectural component owns and guarantees. |
| `ARCH-0002` | Direction | Keep dependency direction aligned with stable behavior. |
| `ARCH-0003` | Invariants | State only architectural invariants that the project truly depends on. |
| `ARCH-0004` | Decisions | Use records when architecture authority materially changes. |


## ARCH-0001 - Ownership

For each major component or package, document the behavior it owns, inputs it trusts, outputs or contracts it guarantees, replaceable dependencies, state classifications, and failure forms that are allowed to cross its boundary. Architecture documentation should describe actual authority, not an aspirational folder diagram.

## ARCH-0002 - Direction

Prefer dependency direction that keeps stable project behavior independent from delivery and infrastructure details when useful. A common shape is interface -> orchestration -> core behavior -> contracts -> adapters. This is a design heuristic, not a mandatory directory layout; small repositories may collapse layers while preserving clear ownership.

## ARCH-0003 - Invariants

Useful invariants may include durable identity independence from temporary external values, separation of source-of-truth state from rebuildable projections, isolation of external effects, duplicate-safe retries, secret containment, and intentional control of implementation-language leakage into stable contracts. Each invariant should be testable or auditable enough to constrain design decisions.

## ARCH-0004 - Decisions

When a proposed change materially alters an invariant, dependency direction, data ownership rule, public contract, or trust boundary, create or revise the appropriate decision record rather than editing the guide as though no decision occurred. Reconcile the accepted outcome back into this guide so it remains the current discoverable authority.
</file>

