# Boundaries

| ID | Topic | Summary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `BNDR-0001` | Ownership | Keep stable behavior separate from delivery and infrastructure details. |
| `BNDR-0002` | Adapters | Use explicit adapters where dependencies vary or fail independently. |
| `BNDR-0003` | Errors | Normalize failures before they escape the boundary that understands them. |
| `BNDR-0004` | Effects | Keep side effects visible at the boundary where they occur. |


## BNDR-0001 - Ownership

Stable project behavior should have an identifiable owner. Framework requests, database rows, vendor SDK objects, process globals, and similar infrastructure values should not become default cross-layer contracts unless that exposure is intentional and documented.

## BNDR-0002 - Adapters

Prefer small explicit interfaces or adapters when a dependency is replaceable, independently testable, failure-prone, external, or governed by a different lifecycle. Avoid abstractions created only for hypothetical portability with no current invariant or testable contract.

## BNDR-0003 - Errors

Translate external or implementation-specific failures into stable project categories before they spread into unrelated layers. Preserve enough underlying evidence for diagnosis without making vendor-specific exception types part of a public contract.

## BNDR-0004 - Effects

Do not hide network, filesystem, process, storage, queue, or other state-changing effects inside APIs that look like pure value transformations. Naming, interfaces, orchestration, and tests should make effect ownership visible.
</file>

