# Configuration

| ID | Topic | Summary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `CONF-0001` | Sources | Classify configuration sources and keep their roles distinct. |
| `CONF-0002` | Precedence | Define deterministic precedence when multiple sources can provide a value. |
| `CONF-0003` | Validation | Validate configuration at a clear boundary with actionable errors. |
| `CONF-0004` | Secrets | Treat secrets as configuration inputs with stricter handling rules. |


## CONF-0001 - Sources

Distinguish non-secret configuration, secrets and credentials, environment discovery, user preferences, generated runtime state, and build-time constants. Do not use one mechanism as an unstructured catch-all for unrelated concerns.

## CONF-0002 - Precedence

If a setting may come from command-line input, files, environment, remote configuration, defaults, or other sources, document the precedence order explicitly. Avoid hidden fallback behavior that makes the effective value difficult to explain.

## CONF-0003 - Validation

Validate required fields, types, ranges, mutually exclusive options, and incompatible combinations before dependent behavior begins. Errors should identify the invalid source and key without leaking secret values.

## CONF-0004 - Secrets

Secret values should not be committed, echoed in normal logs, serialized into generated artifacts, or returned through ordinary diagnostics. Document how secret references are supplied, rotated, and redacted without coupling stable project semantics to a particular secret manager.
</file>

