# Flow

| ID | Topic | Summary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `FLOW-0001` | Classes | Classify important data by authority and lifecycle. |
| `FLOW-0002` | Lineage | Preserve provenance when correctness or rebuildability depends on it. |
| `FLOW-0003` | Transforms | Make consequential transformations explicit and versionable. |
| `FLOW-0004` | Rebuilds | Define what rebuildable means before relying on it. |


## FLOW-0001 - Classes

Distinguish authoritative project state, authoritative external state, immutable evidence, normalized data, derived state, cache, operational metadata, and ephemeral runtime state whenever those categories imply different correctness or lifecycle rules.

## FLOW-0002 - Lineage

Derived data should retain enough lineage to explain which source facts and transformation rules produced it. Do not silently replace source facts with interpreted values. If normalization or projection is lossy, preserve original evidence or document why the loss is safe.

## FLOW-0003 - Transforms

When transformations affect identity, compatibility, hashes, user-visible meaning, persistence, or recovery, define their inputs, outputs, normalization rules, and versioning behavior explicitly enough to reproduce or compare results.

## FLOW-0004 - Rebuilds

State called rebuildable must identify the source inputs, transformation version, required external dependencies, and any exceptions that cannot be reconstructed. A cache or projection is not safely disposable merely because it was labeled derived.
</file>

