# Naming

| ID | Topic | Summary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `NAME-0001` | Meaning | Prefer names that express project meaning rather than implementation accidents. |
| `NAME-0002` | Canonical | Use one canonical term for one concept within an authority scope. |
| `NAME-0003` | Abbreviations | Use abbreviations only when they are standard or improve durable readability. |
| `NAME-0004` | Effects | Name state-changing operations so their effects are visible. |


## NAME-0001 - Meaning

Names should communicate repository or domain meaning at the scope where they are authoritative. Avoid leaking temporary framework, transport, database, or vendor vocabulary into stable concepts unless that dependency is intentionally part of the contract.

## NAME-0002 - Canonical

Aliases may exist at compatibility or adapter boundaries, but current internal guidance should name the concept consistently. If two names represent different lifecycle, ownership, or identity semantics, keep them distinct rather than collapsing them for convenience.

## NAME-0003 - Abbreviations

Prefer descriptive names over project-local shorthand. Fixed governed abbreviations, such as guide content prefixes, should be centrally registered and stable rather than invented independently in each file.

## NAME-0004 - Effects

Use verbs or ecosystem-appropriate forms that communicate creation, mutation, deletion, publication, synchronization, or other effects. Boolean values should read as predicates or state claims where the language ecosystem supports that convention.
</file>

