# Persistence

| ID | Topic | Summary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `PERS-0001` | Classes | Classify persisted data by authority and lifecycle. |
| `PERS-0002` | Identity | Do not persist temporary locators as durable identity. |
| `PERS-0003` | Migrations | Make durable schema changes explicit and testable. |
| `PERS-0004` | Boundaries | Return project representations rather than storage internals. |


## PERS-0001 - Classes

Classify durable values as authoritative, external evidence, normalized, derived, cached, or operational when those distinctions change migration, retention, recovery, or update semantics.

## PERS-0002 - Identity

Temporary URLs, sessions, credentials, process handles, volatile provider tokens, and environment-specific locators should not become durable identity unless the external system explicitly guarantees that property and the repository accepts it as a contract.

## PERS-0003 - Migrations

Migrations should declare source and target versions, ordering, failure behavior, and recovery or rollback expectations. Prefer reversible or otherwise recoverable migration paths where practical, and validate important migrations against representative state.

## PERS-0004 - Boundaries

Persistence boundaries should expose stable project representations and meaningful errors rather than leaking ORM entities, driver rows, file handles, or storage-engine implementation details into unrelated layers.
</file>

