# Portability

| ID | Topic | Summary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `PORT-0001` | Behavior | Stabilize behavior rather than implementation details. |
| `PORT-0002` | Boundaries | Make operating-system and runtime dependencies explicit. |
| `PORT-0003` | Adoption | Do not mistake example knowledge for adopted technology authority. |
| `PORT-0004` | Migration | Compare implementations at stable behavioral boundaries. |


## PORT-0001 - Behavior

Portability means stable project behavior can survive a change in language, framework, storage engine, deployment platform, or adapter without an accidental domain redesign. Useful boundaries include explicit schemas, language-independent fixtures, deterministic serialization, stable error categories, and small adapter interfaces.

## PORT-0002 - Boundaries

Time, randomness, filesystem, network, process, environment, permissions, and external runtime behavior should be isolated or documented when they can vary across supported environments. Do not encode one platform's incidental behavior as universal semantics without an accepted constraint.

## PORT-0003 - Adoption

Language, framework, database, and platform notes under `agents/knowledge/` are non-normative examples or research until deliberately reconciled into a known-scope guide or accepted project record. Governance tooling language does not imply application-language authority.

## PORT-0004 - Migration

During a migration or rewrite, verify equivalent public behavior, schemas, fixtures, persistence effects, and recovery properties rather than requiring private internal structure to match. Implementation-specific optimizations should stay behind the boundary when they are not intended to redefine behavior.
</file>

