# Storage

| ID | Topic | Summary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `STOR-0001` | Ownership | Document what each durable store or artifact owns. |
| `STOR-0002` | Semantics | Do not rely on unspecified storage behavior. |
| `STOR-0003` | Separation | Separate data classes when their lifecycles or authority differ. |
| `STOR-0004` | Recovery | Treat backup and recovery as behavior rather than an afterthought. |


## STOR-0001 - Ownership

For every store or durable artifact, identify its owned data, durability expectations, transaction or atomicity requirements, concurrency model, migration strategy, retention or deletion policy, backup and recovery expectations, and whether its content is authoritative, derived, cached, or append-only evidence.

## STOR-0002 - Semantics

Ordering, isolation, transaction, filesystem, locking, durability, and consistency assumptions that affect correctness must be explicit and testable. Storage-engine defaults are implementation details until the repository deliberately makes them part of a contract.

## STOR-0003 - Separation

Raw or external facts, normalized representations, derived state, caches, and operational metadata should not be conflated when they have different retention, rebuildability, privacy, or correctness requirements.

## STOR-0004 - Recovery

Define the recovery unit, restoration source, integrity evidence, version compatibility, and acceptable loss window when durable state matters. A backup claim should be paired with a plausible restore path and validation method.
</file>

