# Terminology

| ID | Topic | Summary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `TERM-0001` | Definitions | Define repository-specific terms before using them as authority. |
| `TERM-0002` | Distinctions | Keep terms distinct when they represent different semantics. |
| `TERM-0003` | Neutrality | Prefer technology-neutral terms in stable contracts unless technology is part of the contract. |
| `TERM-0004` | Mappings | Document conflicting ecosystem vocabulary explicitly. |


## TERM-0001 - Definitions

A term that constrains architecture, ownership, lifecycle, state, or compatibility should have an explicit meaning in the repository. Do not assume that a familiar word has the same semantics across languages, frameworks, or teams.

## TERM-0002 - Distinctions

Do not merge vocabulary merely because two values look similar. Ownership, lifecycle, identity, source-of-truth status, mutability, or external provenance can justify separate terms even when their implementation representations overlap.

## TERM-0003 - Neutrality

Stable project vocabulary should survive implementation replacement when practical. If an external technology name is itself authoritative, preserve it rather than creating a local synonym that obscures upstream meaning.

## TERM-0004 - Mappings

When upstream systems or languages use different names for equivalent or overlapping concepts, document the mapping and note any semantic mismatch. Adapters may translate vocabulary, but evidence should preserve literal external identifiers where traceability depends on them.
</file>

