# Topology

| ID | Topic | Summary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `TOPO-0001` | Repository | Map the important repository roots without imposing a language layout. |
| `TOPO-0002` | Runtime | Describe processes and services only when the project actually has them. |
| `TOPO-0003` | Packages | Make package ownership and stable dependency direction visible. |
| `TOPO-0004` | External | Identify external systems at the boundary of repository ownership. |


## TOPO-0001 - Repository

Maintain a bounded map of important roots such as `agents/`, application or library source, tests, generated artifacts, and user documentation when they exist. Do not invent roots merely to resemble a template. The topology should make ownership and navigation obvious across languages and project types.

## TOPO-0002 - Runtime

For runtime components, document process or service ownership, communication direction, trust boundaries, persistence boundaries, deployment coupling, lifecycle, and restart behavior. If the project has no runtime topology, state that rather than forcing a service-oriented model.

## TOPO-0003 - Packages

For monorepos or multi-package projects, identify package responsibilities and allowed dependency direction. Cycles that create shared authority should be treated as deliberate design decisions, not accidental topology.

## TOPO-0004 - External

Show external services, runtimes, storage engines, operating-system facilities, or upstream/downstream projects only to the degree needed to understand ownership and coupling. Distinguish what the repository controls from what it merely integrates with.
</file>

