# Workflows

| ID | Topic | Summary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `WKFL-0001` | Shape | Describe important behavior that spans components or effects. |
| `WKFL-0002` | Failures | Model failure, retry, cancellation, and partial completion as first-class paths. |
| `WKFL-0003` | Evidence | Define how successful and failed workflow outcomes are verified. |
| `WKFL-0004` | Diagrams | Use diagrams to clarify behavior, not to replace contracts. |


## WKFL-0001 - Shape

For each significant workflow, document the trigger or actor, preconditions, authoritative inputs, core decisions, state transitions, external effects, duplicate or idempotency semantics, cancellation and retry behavior, and observable completion evidence.

## WKFL-0002 - Failures

A workflow description should identify what can fail, which failures are retryable, what state may already have changed, how cancellation is observed, and how the system reconciles partial progress. Happy-path-only diagrams are insufficient where recovery affects correctness.

## WKFL-0003 - Evidence

Completion should leave bounded evidence appropriate to the workflow, such as committed state, a durable event, returned revision, checksum, report, or testable observable. Evidence should support recovery and audit without forcing every transient detail into durable storage.

## WKFL-0004 - Diagrams

Small flow or sequence diagrams are useful when static topology cannot explain ordering. Show initiators, validation points, state-changing effects, external dependencies, failure or retry paths, and terminal evidence. Keep project vocabulary out of the starter until the consuming repository defines it.
</file>

<file path="agents/knowledge/README.md">
# Governed Knowledge

`agents/knowledge/` stores reusable research, references, and synthesis that may inform work across iterations. Knowledge is normally non-normative: it records **what is known**, not automatically **what the repository must do**.

## Topic layout

Use one directory per stable topic:

```text
agents/knowledge/<topic>/README.md
agents/knowledge/<topic>/NOTE-0001--example-title.md
```

A topic can represent a language, runtime, framework, database, protocol, external service, domain concept, operating-system facility, standard, or other reusable research area.

## Starter examples

This package retains only the TypeScript knowledge-topic README because the target is a Chrome extension:

- `typescript/README.md`

It illustrates organization and does not itself make any framework, extension platform version, or build tool authoritative. Add governed notes only when concrete technology decisions are made.

## Governed notes

Create a `NOTE-XXXX` when research is reusable enough to preserve source kind, freshness, provenance, and later review information. Use `agents/templates/NOTE.md` or the optional knowledge manager.

Example path:

```text
agents/knowledge/http/NOTE-0001--retry-semantics.md
```

## Promotion

A note does not silently create repository policy.

- bounded verified fact with repository consequence -> `FINDING` when durable evidence is useful;
- durable architectural or technical choice -> `ADR` when a decision record is useful;
- known-scope engineering rule -> reconcile into the matching `agents/guides/<scope>.md` after the necessary evidence/decision process;
- durable engineering rule with unresolved guide scope -> temporary `CONVENTION-XXXX` until classification is clear.

See `agents/governance/artifacts.md` for the complete routing model.

## Freshness

Technology and external-service knowledge can become stale. Record `last_verified_at` and, when useful, `review_after`. Historical knowledge may remain valuable when clearly labeled historical.

## Helper

`python3 agents/scripts/knowledge-manager.py` may allocate `NOTE-XXXX`, render the canonical note template, validate note metadata, and report notes whose freshness is unknown or whose `review_after` is due. The Markdown note remains authoritative; the manager stores no hidden state and never promotes a note automatically.
</file>

