# AUDIT-0012 — v0.0.28 ROADMAP-0003 STEP-10 Retention Quota Migration and Restart Recovery

## Audit objective

Determine whether the completed UI now has bounded automatic retention, explicit quota/write-failure behavior, resumable logical migration, and correct service-worker/browser-restart recovery without broadening permission, storage-topology or canonical-deletion authority.

## Accepted implementation

- Physical IndexedDB remains v1 with the existing five stores/indexes. Logical durable authority advances to v2 through a resumable/idempotent v1→v2 migration that seeds retention/recovery metadata.
- Settings schema advances to v2 with bounded terminal-history retention; predecessor v1 Settings migrate in place to defaults rather than being rejected/reset. Default policy is 30 days / 50 terminal WatchSessions.
- Retention is index-driven and excludes active Watch. On startup/session-end/policy-change/manual execution it removes only terminal history already outside the configured age/count policy, including the corresponding observations/PageFieldSnapshots/lifecycle record.
- Approximate `navigator.storage.estimate()` signals classify normal/warning/critical state. Warning/critical pressure may run the same configured policy early but cannot broaden the deletion set to in-policy canonical history.
- IndexedDB write failures are normalized, including quota failures. A failed durable observation commit ends Watch with `storage_failure`, clears active session authority, suspends capture, and surfaces storage degradation.
- Terminal pending observation evidence is preserved across same-browser service-worker interruption and replayed after durable writes recover. The product does not claim the observation committed until IndexedDB succeeds.
- Browser restart still clears browser-lifetime session coordination and never auto-resumes Watch. Existing durable history/definitions/preferences remain.
- Existing STEP-08 Clear history/all-local actions remain explicit, user-confirmed, and blocked while Watch is active; automatic retention never inherits their broader deletion scope.
- No new browser permission, `unlimitedStorage`, physical IndexedDB store/index, remote telemetry or scraper artifact tier was introduced.

## Repository-controlled validation before final recovery projection

- All 12 repository TypeScript project configurations compile with `--noEmit`.
- Complete repository-controlled product suite: **183/183 tests pass** on the reconciled final semantic candidate.
- Focused persistence **10/10**, Watch **23/23**, observations **15/15**, control-plane/Settings **22/22**, UI **5/5** pass.
- After version/governance reconciliation, all 12 TypeScript configurations, 183/183 product tests, 94/94 managed records, 0 knowledge notes and workspace audit pass. A deterministic pre-promotion portable projection was cold-extracted and independently reran all 12 TypeScript configurations, 183/183 product tests, 94/94 records, archive-safety and workspace-equivalence checks successfully. The final sealed artifact must repeat the same recovery checks before publication.

## Environment/inherited lanes

Native selected `vue-tsc`, WXT packaged-browser and assistive-technology execution remain `deferred_environment` until the selected dependencies/browser environment are hydrated. The archive-derived initialization root-identity mismatch and portable historical `changes.diff` omissions remain explicit inherited non-passes and are not STEP-10 failures.

## Conclusion

STEP-10 is accepted at the exact semantic source/governance level with pre-promotion cold recovery proven; final sealed-artifact revalidation remains a publication gate rather than additional product scope. ROADMAP-0003 enters Phase P5 at 1/3; only STEP-11 / v0.0.29 is authorized next.
