# FINDING-0007 - Debugger/CDP Is Too Powerful and Inflexible for Baseline Watch

## Source fact

Chrome's `debugger` permission cannot be optional. `chrome.debugger` exposes the CDP Network domain, whose stable protocol can retrieve request post data and response bodies and observe cache/WebSocket events. Chrome also terminates the debugger session when DevTools is opened for the attached tab and requires target/session handling for out-of-process child frames.

## Source / evidence

REFERENCE-0004 revision 2 and the STEP-01 research-data capture from current `chrome.debugger`, `chrome.permissions`, and stable CDP Network documentation.

## Interpretation

Debugger/CDP solves some completeness/data-access gaps but forces a sensitive standing named permission and a materially larger capability surface than the baseline resource-list purpose needs. Its lifecycle also competes with normal DevTools use.

## Repository implication

Do not make debugger/CDP the initial baseline Watch capture plane. Preserve it as an explicit deferred advanced capability that can be reconsidered only when later evidence shows a requirement that the narrower baseline cannot satisfy.

## Confidence / limitations

High for the documented permission and detach behavior. No runtime probe is performed in this research-only step.

## Follow-up

MATRIX-0003 and ROADMAP-0001 STEP-05; future advanced capture requires separate authorization.

## Revision history

