# FINDING-0008 - Page Resource Timing Is Supplementary, Not Canonical Network Authority

## Source fact

W3C Resource Timing exposes page resource entries but deliberately restricts cross-origin timing/detail unless Timing-Allow-Origin conditions are satisfied; user agents may still zero selected size values. Chrome `webRequest` separately documents that renderer in-memory-cache hits can be invisible to it.

## Source / evidence

REFERENCE-0004 revision 2 and the STEP-01 research-data capture.

## Interpretation

Page-visible timing/DOM evidence may be valuable for correlation and for explaining some resources absent from webRequest, but it lacks the complete request/response authority needed to serve as the canonical sniffer source.

## Repository implication

Treat page Resource Timing/DOM evidence as an optional supplemental adapter whose results are explicitly sourced and deduplicated against canonical network observations. Do not use it to inflate capture-completeness claims.

## Confidence / limitations

High for standards-level cross-origin limits and documented webRequest cache behavior. Exact useful supplement behavior remains an implementation-phase topic after architecture synthesis.

## Follow-up

MATRIX-0003; STEP-02 observation identity; STEP-03 page-context semantics.

