# FINDING-0009 - Watch Is Tab-Scoped Durable Intent with Document Epochs

## Source fact

Chrome may terminate MV3 extension service workers after idle periods and loses their globals. Chrome also defines no ordering between `webRequest` and `webNavigation`, while current request/navigation events expose document IDs that distinguish documents across frame navigation.

## Source / evidence

REFERENCE-0004 revision 2 and the STEP-01 research-data capture.

## Interpretation

A boolean held in a service-worker global cannot be Watch authority, and a tab/frame ID alone is not sufficient for page/document attribution. Watch intent and document attribution must be separate identities.

## Repository implication

Use an application-level `WatchSession` hypothesis: one explicit session targets one tab and may continue across same-tab navigations until Stop/close/permission loss. Each new committed page is a document epoch; observations retain `documentId`/`frameId` when available, and ambiguous navigation races remain explicitly unattributed. Worker restart reconciles existing intent; browser/profile restart does not silently re-arm Watch.

## Confidence / limitations

High for lifecycle/document facts. Exact durable storage and Side Panel status UX are deliberately deferred to STEP-04.

## Follow-up

CONSTRAINT-0003, MATRIX-0004, STEP-02 observation identity, STEP-03 PageContext, STEP-04 persistence/UI.

