# FINDING-0015 - Namespaced Missing Aware Tokens Are Safer Than Inheriting JDownloader Dynamic Variable Syntax

## Source fact

JDownloader exposes `<jd:...>` dynamic variables including plugin/environment state and currently converts a missing plugin property to empty string. Cat Catch exposes `${...}` values and chained transforms, including cookies/raw data in some contexts.

## Source / evidence

REFERENCE-0002 revision 3; REFERENCE-0003 revision 2; official Cat Catch/JDownloader docs refreshed 2026-08-19.

## Interpretation

crsniffer needs the ergonomic idea, not either exact namespace or sensitive value surface. Empty-string coercion also destroys the distinction between "present but empty" and "unavailable".

## Repository implication

Use crsniffer namespaced `${resource.*}`/`${watch.*}` tokens, reserve `${page.*}`/`${field.*}` for STEP-03, retain a distinct Missing sentinel, require explicit `default` handling, whitelist safe transforms, and exclude cookies/credentials/raw payload/environment/plugin-property namespaces.

## Confidence / limitations

High for the privacy/missing-value boundary; exact parser spelling remains an architecture-contract candidate until STEP-05.

## Follow-up

MATRIX-0007, CONSTRAINT-0004, ROADMAP-0001 STEP-03/05.

