# FINDING-0017 - Top-Level Page Context Must Be Separate from Network Initiating Document Identity

## Source fact

Chrome exposes request `documentId`/`parentDocumentId` for the document issuing a network request, while `webNavigation` separately identifies the tab's main-frame document (`frameId=0`) and its document lifecycle. A request may originate in a child frame rather than the visible top-level document.

## Source / evidence

REFERENCE-0004 revision 4; current Chrome `webRequest` and `webNavigation` references; REFERENCE-0008 donor comparison.

## Interpretation

The visible page whose title/fields a user expects to use for naming is not always the same document that initiated a resource request. Treating request `documentId` as the page-field identity would incorrectly deny or mis-bind top-page fields for subframe resources; treating tab ID/current URL as identity would permit stale cross-document enrichment.

## Repository implication

Model a top-level `PageContext` separately from initiating-document evidence. Candidate identity is `WatchSessionId + tabId + topDocumentId`; each ResourceObservation retains initiating `documentId` and a separate captured/resolved `topDocumentId` attribution status. Baseline page mappings target the top-level page. A subframe resource may use top-page fields only when its top-document attribution is reliable; otherwise page/field enrichment is Missing rather than guessed.

## Confidence / limitations

High for the identity separation. Exact frame-ancestry bookkeeping is an implementation concern for the successor roadmap and must preserve the fail-closed attribution rule.

## Follow-up

MATRIX-0009, MATRIX-0010, CONSTRAINT-0005, ROADMAP-0001 STEP-05.

