# FINDING-0018 - Document ID Is the Stale-Result Authority While Same-Document Navigation Needs a Page Revision

## Source fact

Chrome documents that frame IDs can persist across navigations while `documentId` changes when a frame loads a new document. History API and fragment updates keep the document but emit updated URL events; BFCache/prerender lifecycle can also preserve a document ID across lifecycle changes.

## Source / evidence

REFERENCE-0004 revision 4; Chrome `webNavigation` current documentation.

## Interpretation

`tabId + frameId + URL` is not a safe stale-result key. Conversely, document ID alone does not express same-document URL/history revisions that can change DOM-dependent field meaning.

## Repository implication

Use `documentId` as the hard cross-document stale boundary and maintain a monotonic `pageRevision` within a top-level PageContext for same-document history/fragment transitions and explicit refresh invalidation. A new committed top-level document ID immediately invalidates prior field snapshots for future enrichment. Same-document navigation invalidates reusable field snapshots even though the document ID remains stable.

## Confidence / limitations

High for document ID and same-document event semantics. Arbitrary DOM mutation has no universal navigation event, so `pageRevision` is not claimed to be a complete DOM-change detector.

## Follow-up

MATRIX-0009, CONSTRAINT-0005, FINDING-0019.

