# FINDING-0019 - Page Fields Should Evaluate On Demand Against an Exact Document and Produce Immutable Snapshots

## Source fact

Chrome `scripting.executeScript()` can target an exact `documentId` and returns injection results carrying `documentId`; Chrome navigation events do not provide a universal signal for arbitrary DOM mutation, and `onDOMContentLoaded` is not fired for every BFCache restoration.

## Source / evidence

REFERENCE-0004 revision 4; Chrome `scripting` and `webNavigation`; REFERENCE-0009; REFERENCE-0008 donor comparison.

## Interpretation

A continuously cached page-field model would either require an always-on mutation observer or make freshness claims it cannot prove. Both are poor defaults for a network sniffer whose page mapping is secondary enrichment.

## Repository implication

Evaluate referenced page mappings on demand when a live rule/template or explicit Preview actually requires `page.*`/`field.*`, target the exact active top-level document, and return an immutable `PageFieldSnapshot`. The snapshot records PageContext identity/revision, frozen mapping identity/revision/hash, evaluation time, typed field values and structural diagnostics. If the exact document is gone or changes before consumption, discard the result and surface Missing/stale; never rerun against whatever document is now current.

## Confidence / limitations

High for on-demand exact-document direction; performance caching can be reconsidered in STEP-04/ROADMAP-0002 only if it preserves the same freshness contract.

## Follow-up

MATRIX-0009, MATRIX-0010, CONSTRAINT-0005.

