# FINDING-0020 - CSS and XPath Need Typed Snapshot Normalization with Missing-Aware Read and Cardinality

## Source fact

DOM CSS selection is node-oriented and `querySelectorAll()` is static. XPath can return nodes or scalar string/number/boolean values; ordered node iterators are mutation-sensitive while ordered snapshots are stable to iterate. Missing attributes are represented distinctly from empty attribute values.

## Source / evidence

REFERENCE-0009 primary DOM/XPath/HTML standards; REFERENCE-0008 read-only donor selector/read/cardinality evidence.

## Interpretation

A single "selector -> string" abstraction would discard XPath scalar types, blur missing versus falsey values, and create mutation-sensitive node iteration hazards. Conversely, exposing every DOM object/property or serialized markup would unnecessarily expand security/privacy complexity.

## Repository implication

Normalize CSS results to ordered static node arrays and XPath node results to ordered snapshots before the semantic boundary; preserve XPath scalar string/number/boolean values. Candidate read modes are `text_content`, `inner_text`, `attribute(name)`, direct primitive `property(name)`, plus `raw` for XPath scalar results. Defer `inner_html`/`outer_html` from the baseline. Preserve cardinality `first`, `optional_one`, `exact_one`, `many`, and preserve Missing separately from null/empty/false/zero. Ordered fallback advances only on Missing or explicit candidate rejection; selector/read/cardinality/transform/validation errors do not silently fall through.

## Confidence / limitations

High for selector/result normalization and Missing behavior. Exact implementation typing/bounds remain successor concerns.

## Follow-up

MATRIX-0008, CONSTRAINT-0005, ROADMAP-0001 STEP-05.

