# FINDING-0022 - Page and Field Token Provenance Must Fail Closed on Stale or Unattributed Context

## Source fact

Chrome exposes document identity across webRequest/webNavigation/scripting/message sender surfaces, but it explicitly defines no ordering between webRequest and webNavigation events. Exact-document execution can return a document ID for result validation.

## Source / evidence

REFERENCE-0004 revision 4; current Chrome documentation; FINDING-0017 through FINDING-0019.

## Interpretation

A resource can arrive near navigation boundaries or from a child frame where top-document attribution is unavailable. Filling `${page.*}` or `${field.*}` from the tab's current page in those cases would create plausible but false metadata.

## Repository implication

`page.*`/`field.*` become immutable source facts only when bound to a PageFieldSnapshot whose topDocumentId/pageRevision is valid for the ResourceObservation's captured top-page attribution. Unknown/ambiguous/stale context yields Missing with a structural reason code. `page.url`, `page.origin`, and `page.title` come from the exact PageContext snapshot; `${field.<slug>}` uses a frozen referenced mapping. Packagizer actions remain unable to mutate these source facts.

## Confidence / limitations

High for fail-closed provenance. Resource-to-top-document attribution implementation must be proved later under real browser navigation races.

## Follow-up

MATRIX-0009, MATRIX-0010, CONSTRAINT-0005, ROADMAP-0001 STEP-05.

