# FINDING-0025 - Persistence Should Separate Durable IndexedDB Session Coordination and Small Local Preferences

## Source fact

IndexedDB is available in extension service workers and is appropriate for structured durable data. `chrome.storage.session` survives worker termination but is cleared on browser restart/reload/update. `chrome.storage.local` is durable but quota-bounded, while `storage.sync` is substantially smaller.

## Source / evidence

REFERENCE-0012 and current Chrome storage/IndexedDB documentation.

## Interpretation

No one storage mechanism matches crsniffer's high-volume observation history, restart-sensitive active-session coordination and small preference state.

## Repository implication

Use IndexedDB as candidate canonical durable authority for reusable definitions, WatchSession records, ResourceObservations, evaluated PageFieldSnapshots where required, bounded diagnostics/history and schema/meta. Use `storage.session` only for ephemeral active-session coordination/presentation hints that may survive worker shutdown but MUST disappear on browser restart, aligning with CONSTRAINT-0003. Use `storage.local` only for small preferences/install/UI metadata. Reject `storage.sync` for observation/rule history and reject `unlimitedStorage` as baseline. Exact object stores, schema version and retention durations remain STEP-05/implementation work.

## Confidence / limitations

High for storage-role separation; quota behavior and migration mechanics require implementation validation.

## Follow-up

MATRIX-0012, CONSTRAINT-0006, ROADMAP-0001 STEP-05.

