# FINDING-0029 - Accessibility Localization Privacy and Recovery Are Architecture Contracts Not Polish

## Source fact

Chrome accessibility guidance and WCAG 2.2 require keyboard operability, visible focus and reflow/focus/status semantics. Chrome Web Store policy requires accurate privacy disclosure and limits browsing-activity collection to the prominently described user-facing purpose. WXT supports browser-native i18n workflows.

## Source / evidence

REFERENCE-0011, REFERENCE-0012, CONSTRAINT-0003 through CONSTRAINT-0005.

## Interpretation

These requirements affect component selection, navigation, live-update announcements, persisted diagnostics and string architecture from the first implementation scaffold; bolting them on at release time would create avoidable rework and privacy drift.

## Repository implication

Use semantic/native controls first; keyboard-complete interactions; visible non-color-only focus/status; WCAG 2.2 AA candidate target with 320 CSS-px reflow and adequate target sizing; respect reduced motion. Announce Watch/Stop, permission/error and summarized batch-count changes via appropriate status semantics, not every resource row. Externalize all user-facing strings from first implementation using browser i18n/WXT-compatible localization; browser-language selection is baseline, manual language override deferred. Diagnostics remain local/redacted by default and do not persist raw URLs/page-field/token values unless an explicit user action requires them.

## Confidence / limitations

High for architecture floor; formal packaged assistive-technology certification remains later release evidence.

## Follow-up

MATRIX-0013, CONSTRAINT-0006, ROADMAP-0001 STEP-05.

