# REFERENCE-0004 - Chrome MV3 Network Observation and Runtime Authority

## Purpose

Preserve current first-party authority for Chrome MV3 network observation, runtime host access, debugger/CDP instrumentation, navigation/document identity, service-worker lifecycle, messaging trust boundaries, and Chrome Web Store browsing-data policy.

## Referenced material

- Chrome `webRequest`: https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/reference/api/webRequest
- Chrome `activeTab`: https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/develop/concepts/activeTab
- Chrome `permissions`: https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/reference/api/permissions
- Chrome match patterns: https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/develop/concepts/match-patterns
- Chrome `debugger`: https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/reference/api/debugger
- Stable Chrome DevTools Protocol Network 1.3: https://chromedevtools.github.io/devtools-protocol/1-3/Network/
- Extension service-worker lifecycle: https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/develop/concepts/service-workers/lifecycle
- Chrome `webNavigation`: https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/reference/api/webNavigation
- Chrome extension messaging: https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/develop/concepts/messaging
- Chrome Web Store Limited Use: https://developer.chrome.com/docs/webstore/program-policies/limited-use
- W3C Resource Timing: https://www.w3.org/TR/resource-timing/
- Chrome content-script globs: https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/reference/manifest/content-scripts

## Current facts retained for STEP-01

- Normal `webRequest` observation remains available in MV3; comprehensive subresource observation requires host authority for both the requested URL and initiator.
- Renderer in-memory-cache requests are documented as invisible to `webRequest`; WebSocket request observation covers the handshake rather than message traffic.
- `activeTab` is temporary/user-mediated but its `webRequest` authority is described for the active tab's main-frame origin, so it cannot be treated as blanket third-party CDN authority.
- Broad host patterns can be declared as optional host permissions and requested from a user gesture. Chrome explicitly states that `debugger` cannot be optional.
- `chrome.debugger` can access the allowed CDP Network domain, but DevTools opening on the target tab can terminate the debugger session; current target/session behavior also requires child-target care.
- MV3 service-worker globals are lost when the worker shuts down; durable application state must not depend on process memory.
- Chrome defines no ordering between `webRequest` and `webNavigation`; `documentId` distinguishes documents where supplied while `frameId` may survive document changes.
- Content/page-originated messages are less trusted than privileged extension code and require validation/sanitization.
- Chrome Web Store Limited Use permits browsing-activity use only as required for the prominently described user-facing feature.
- W3C Resource Timing is a useful page-side supplement but cross-origin timing detail is constrained and it is not equivalent to extension request/response authority.
- Each `requestId` is stable for one logical request lifecycle, while redirect/authentication can map multiple physical HTTP requests to the same web request; URL alone therefore cannot be raw observation identity.
- `ResourceType` describes how a request is used; generic `media` is not synonymous with video.
- Chrome content-script `include_globs`/`exclude_globs` use simple `*`/`?` wildcard semantics and are distinct from structured permission/match-pattern syntax.
- `webNavigation` documents `documentId` as unique per document while `frameId` can persist across navigations; History API/fragment events can update URL within the same document, BFCache restore may omit DOMContentLoaded, and webRequest/webNavigation have no defined cross-API ordering.
- `chrome.scripting` can target exact `documentIds` and returns `documentId` in injection results; its default execution world is ISOLATED.
- Runtime MessageSender can expose documentId/documentLifecycle/frame/origin/URL/tab evidence, but documentLifecycle reflects connection-creation state rather than a permanent freshness guarantee.
- webRequest exposes document/parent-document/frame evidence where available, allowing explicit top-document attribution states rather than tab-URL guessing.

## Provenance

Chrome for Developers, Chrome DevTools Protocol, and W3C documentation retrieved/refreshed 2026-08-19 through ROADMAP-0001 STEP-03. Detailed source-fact/repository-interpretation separation is preserved in `agents/records/research-data/20260819/20260819T102100Z_v0001_step01-network-observation-permission-watch-security-authority.md`.

## Usage constraints

This reference is freshness-sensitive source authority. It does not by itself choose the implementation architecture. MATRIX-0003/MATRIX-0004 record STEP-01 research dispositions; final normative acceptance remains STEP-05/ADR work.

## Related records

FINDING-0006 through FINDING-0010, CONSTRAINT-0003, MATRIX-0003, MATRIX-0004, ROADMAP-0001 STEP-01/STEP-04/STEP-05.

## Asset inventory

Source URLs plus governed research-data synthesis; no executable browser probe is part of the research-only iteration.

