# REFERENCE-0017 — ROADMAP-0003 STEP-07 reusable-definition lifecycle and technical-affordance authority

## Reference scope

Freeze the current evidence used to unify saved-definition lifecycle without changing durable authority or creating new portability formats.

## WAI interaction authority

Reviewed the current WAI APG Combobox and Disclosure patterns. Native `<select>` remains the Crsniffer default because no current library-scale evidence justifies taking on a custom combobox keyboard/focus contract. Native `<details>/<summary>` is accepted for secondary support-only identity/schema inspection.

## Repository authority

`DefinitionCatalog` remains the only durable saved-definition authority. Loading copies values into disposable UI state; selection never mutates or starts Watch. Save as creates a new identity, Update is expected-revision fenced, Reset reloads the source, Duplicate creates an unsaved copy, and Delete removes only an exact current revision that is neither protected nor referenced by another current saved definition. Immutable WatchSession snapshots remain historical authority and are never rewritten or treated as live delete references.

For custom Watch deletion, only an exact now-orphaned hidden resource-filter child may be cleaned; unrelated/shared filters remain. Successful delete operations emit ordinary definition/configuration invalidation hints; read-only inspection never does.

## Read-only donor disposition

Then-latest promoted `crscraper v0.0.51` was inspected read-only (SHA-256 `350df460e74f1e022ab899276704c0bcbd6eb34dd6737822bc2ccddecb0e9f33`). Crsniffer adopts its coherent lifecycle/state/provenance principles but rejects its STEP-09 Load/Set/Import/Export portability workspace for this iteration because ROADMAP-0003 STEP-07 expressly forbids a new public import/export format. Dedicated donor Library/search UI and scraper-specific domains are also not copied.

## Scope implication

STEP-07 may add shared lifecycle helpers, reverse-reference deletion guards, contextual Duplicate/Delete actions and collapsed technical details to already accepted editors. It does not authorize Settings, new persistence stores/indexes, new browser permissions, public JSON/file portability, or changes to WatchSession/evaluator/PageContext semantics.

