# REFERENCE-0019 — ROADMAP-0003 STEP-11 browser permission security privacy and packaged-host authority

## Reference scope

Freeze the current first-party Chrome authority, Crsniffer trust/privacy invariants, and read-only donor disposition used for ROADMAP-0003 STEP-11.

## Chrome authority

- Runtime optional host permission remains the accepted least-privilege mechanism for hosts not needed at install time.
- `webRequest` visibility depends on host authority for both the requested URL and initiator, so Crsniffer retains broad HTTP(S) as **optional only** to observe cross-origin subresources during an explicit Watch.
- MV3 extension pages retain self-only executable-code CSP. STEP-11 explicitly declares `script-src 'self'; object-src 'self';` and adds no remote-code authority.
- Message/Port input is not trusted merely because site permission exists. Privileged Crsniffer control-plane callers must authenticate as the current packaged extension ID/origin before dispatch.
- `incognito: not_allowed` is the accepted privacy posture until a separately governed incognito storage/retention contract exists.
- Extension storage is not secret storage; Crsniffer minimizes sensitive persisted data and never adds external telemetry in this step.

## Crsniffer STEP-11 authority

- Required permissions stay exactly `storage`, `webRequest`, `webNavigation`, and `scripting`.
- Optional host permissions stay exactly broad HTTP/HTTPS patterns; install-time host permissions remain absent.
- `activeTab`, broad `tabs`, `debugger`, `downloads`, `nativeMessaging`, `offscreen`, `unlimitedStorage`, clipboard permissions, externally connectable web surfaces, content scripts, and web-accessible resource surfaces are not introduced.
- Privileged Side Panel one-shot messages and Ports are accepted only from the same Crsniffer extension origin/ID.
- Page assistance remains exact-document and `ISOLATED`; site access never substitutes for PageContext document/revision trust.
- HTTP(S) observation URLs preserve resource path/query identity but drop URL username/password before pending/durable state. Non-HTTP(S) payload-bearing URLs retain only their scheme.
- Sensitive request/response headers, bodies, cookies, credentials, raw document IDs and request IDs remain outside normal observation detail/explanation/diagnostic UI.
- Permission loss, tab closure, navigation/document staleness and malformed/untrusted messaging fail closed under existing Watch/Page Field/control-plane laws.

## Donor disposition

Then-latest promoted `crscraper v0.0.54` was inspected read-only (ZIP SHA-256 `c31352bf556d9fe7dce43b34fa04922c65ec6ebcbc5fe70a88dfa1d6a69d5ca8`). Its v0.0.54 STEP-12 shell/Settings delta explicitly introduced no permission-authority change; retained security records therefore remain the relevant donor evidence. Its permission-as-current-capability, sender/document trust composition, exact-document isolated scripting, self-only CSP and honest packaged-host deferral patterns are adopted/adapted. Its Portal/Run exact-origin topology and scraper-specific navigation/export/storage model are not copied because Crsniffer's `webRequest` observation must preserve optional cross-origin subresource coverage.

## Environment disposition

Chromium 144.0.7559.96 is installed, but the selected WXT/vue-tsc package environment is not hydrated: there is no `node_modules`, no package lock, and no WXT CLI. Repository-controlled security/static/unit/integration proof can pass, while actual generated-package MV3 host loading, real permission prompt UX and extension-host CSP/service-worker behavior remain `deferred_environment`. Browser availability alone is not packaged-product proof.

## Scope implication

STEP-11 authorizes permission/trust/privacy/CSP hardening and certification only. It does not authorize dependency hydration/lockfile changes, STEP-12 capacity/performance hardening, STEP-13 package/install/store work, or STEP-14 release closure.

