# v0.0.2 STEP-02 — Resource Filtering, Packagizer Rule, Token, and Preview Authority

## Scope and method

This research-only capture separates external source facts from crsniffer interpretation. It uses current first-party Chrome documentation, WHATWG URL/MIME standards, the IANA media-type registry, official JDownloader Packagizer support documentation, and current Cat Catch project documentation/release evidence. No donor/runtime code, browser harness, packet capture, benchmark, extension build, or executable prototype is used.

## Source facts

### Chrome `webRequest`

Source: https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/reference/api/webRequest (retrieved 2026-08-19).

- Every observed web request has a `requestId` unique within a browser session and extension context; it remains constant for the request lifecycle.
- Chrome explicitly notes that several physical HTTP requests may map to one web request in redirect or authentication cases.
- `onHeadersReceived` may occur multiple times due to redirects/authentication; `onBeforeRedirect` exposes redirect transitions; `onResponseStarted` occurs after status/response headers are available and the first response byte arrives; `onCompleted`/`onErrorOccurred` are terminal events.
- `ResourceType` describes how a requested resource is used. It contains `image` and a generic `media` type; `media` does not mean "video".
- `RequestFilter` can pre-filter by URL patterns, request types, tab and window.
- Chrome does not expose renderer in-memory-cache requests to `webRequest`, so captured request history cannot be claimed complete.

### Chrome globs versus match patterns

Sources:
- https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/reference/manifest/content-scripts
- https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/develop/concepts/match-patterns

Chrome content-script `include_globs`/`exclude_globs` use `*` for any-length text including empty and `?` for one character. They are applied as inclusion/exclusion refinements. Chrome extension match patterns are a different structured language with scheme/host/path constraints. Therefore a product-facing resource glob syntax should not be called or treated as a permission match pattern.

### URL and MIME standards

Sources:
- https://url.spec.whatwg.org/
- https://mimesniff.spec.whatwg.org/
- https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types/media-types.xhtml

The WHATWG URL Standard defines interoperable parsing/serialization and allows fragment-excluding serialization/equality. Query data remains part of the URL. The MIME Sniffing Standard defines the MIME essence and groups `image/*` as image; audio/video MIME groups include `audio/*`, `video/*`, and `application/ogg`. IANA currently registers `application/vnd.apple.mpegurl` for HLS and `application/dash+xml` for DASH.

crsniffer's baseline does not inspect response bodies for MIME sniffing because CONSTRAINT-0003 excludes response-body capture. Classification must therefore remain evidence-based and tolerate unknown/ambiguous results.

### JDownloader Packagizer

Source: https://support.jdownloader.org/en/knowledgebase/article/what-is-the-packagizer (page updated 2025-07-10; retrieved 2026-08-19).

- Packagizer modifies link/package parameters based on already-known parameters.
- Rules affect newly added links; existing LinkGrabber/download-list items are not retroactively changed.
- Rules are ordered. Higher rules can modify filename/package data that affects later rules, and a rule can stop processing further rules.
- Packagizer offers an explicit Test flow.
- Dynamic variables can be used in set-fields, including plugin properties, current package values, environment variables and other JDownloader-specific state.
- A missing plugin property currently resolves to an empty string.
- Rules can be imported/exported.

### Cat Catch 2.7.2

Sources:
- https://github.com/xifangczy/cat-catch/releases (2.7.2 latest at retrieval time; released 2026-07-29, commit `b7383b9`)
- https://o2bmm.gitbook.io/cat-catch/docs/settings
- https://o2bmm.gitbook.io/cat-catch/docs/tag
- https://o2bmm.gitbook.io/cat-catch/docs/preview

Cat Catch exposes configurable resource-type matching, URL blocking/wildcards, list filtering/preview, resource deduplication options, and a `${...}` token language. Its token system includes URL/page/file values and transforms such as slice, replace, regex extraction, existence/default behavior, encoding and case conversion. It also exposes cookies/raw resource data/user-agent-related values for some workflows. Those sensitive tokens are evidence of possible UX breadth, not authority for crsniffer: CONSTRAINT-0003 excludes credential-bearing material and raw payload capture from baseline.

## Repository interpretation and proposed STEP-02 contracts

### 1. Raw observation identity is request-trace identity

`ResourceObservation` should represent one logical Chrome request trace inside one WatchSession. Candidate identity is `(watch_session_id, request_id)`. Redirect/auth events append ordered lifecycle facts/hops to that trace; a redirect URL is not a new raw observation merely because the URL changed. A later request with a different `requestId`, even for the same URL, is a separate observation.

Raw observation history is immutable. User-facing duplicate folding is a separate projection rather than deletion or identity collapse.

A candidate grouping key is `(watch_session_id, document_epoch_or_unknown, canonical_final_url_without_fragment)`. It preserves query bytes/order as parsed/serialized by the URL Standard, because query mutation or sorting can change signed/cache-busting URLs. The projection should retain occurrence count plus first/latest observation IDs and expose conflicts instead of erasing them. Cross-document folding is not a default.

### 2. Classification preserves evidence, precedence, and uncertainty

Candidate semantic classes:

- `image`
- `video`
- `stream_manifest`
- `audio`
- `other`
- `unknown`

Evidence should remain independently visible: parsed MIME essence, Chrome `ResourceType`, final URL extension/path, status/response metadata, and classification reason. Proposed precedence:

1. recognized explicit MIME evidence;
2. registered stream-manifest MIME (`application/vnd.apple.mpegurl`, `application/dash+xml`);
3. Chrome usage type as corroborating/fallback evidence (`image` is useful; generic `media` is not sufficient for video);
4. a bounded extension table only when MIME is missing, generic or unrecognized.

Explicit contradictory MIME beats extension fallback. Generic `media` with no decisive MIME/extension remains `unknown`/ambiguous rather than becoming video. No body sniffing is added.

Default **Images** selects `image`. Default **Videos** selects `video` plus `stream_manifest`; it does not automatically select `audio` or every Chrome `media` request.

### 3. Filtering is admission, not merely display hiding

The default positive lanes are Images + Videos. Optional include globs form another positive lane. Proposed admission formula:

`(matches any selected type preset OR matches any include glob) AND NOT matches any exclude glob`

- selected type presets are ORed;
- include globs are ORed;
- type and include-glob lanes are ORed;
- exclude globs are ORed and veto final admission;
- if there are no positive lanes (no selected presets and no include globs), nothing is admitted;
- users can disable default presets and rely only on globs for arbitrary resource kinds.

This is a persistence/privacy boundary: enough transient metadata may be inspected to classify/filter, but unmatched resource records are not retained as normal history merely to make UI filters work.

### 4. Dedicated resource-glob v1 grammar

Use a small whole-string glob over the canonical URL serialization, not Chrome permission match-pattern syntax:

- `*` = zero or more characters;
- `?` = exactly one character;
- `\\*`, `\\?`, `\\\\` escape literals; trailing escape is invalid;
- case-sensitive matching; URL parser canonicalization naturally normalizes scheme/host while path/query remain case-sensitive;
- full-string match; users use leading/trailing `*` for contains-like behavior;
- no `**`, brace expansion, character classes, extglob syntax, capture groups or regex mode in baseline v1;
- invalid patterns are rejected before a WatchSession can freeze them.

The same safe glob operator can be used as a string condition in Packagizer rules. Regex may be reconsidered later but is not needed to satisfy the initial product floor.

### 5. Packagizer adapts ordered rules but rejects hidden source mutation

Saved rule definitions are mutable configuration. Starting Watch freezes an immutable effective filter/rule/template snapshot for that WatchSession. Editing a saved definition affects future sessions, not an already-running session or past history.

Rules evaluate top-to-bottom. Enabled rules only. Conditions in one rule are ANDed. All rules read the same immutable **source token context** from the admitted resource/Watch facts. Actions write only to a separate **derived projection** (`filename`, `package_name`, `relative_path`/group labels or later accepted fields). Earlier rule actions do not silently change the source context seen by later conditions. Later matched actions may intentionally overwrite earlier derived fields, and preview must show each assignment. `stop_processing` stops later rules only after a successfully applied matched rule.

This retains JDownloader's useful ordering/stop mental model but rejects its hidden coupling where higher rule mutations can make lower conditions change truth value.

Baseline conditions are declarative: exists/missing; equals/not-equals; contains/starts-with/ends-with; the same safe glob operator; and typed numeric comparisons for numeric facts. No arbitrary JavaScript/eval/plugin script execution.

### 6. Namespaced tokens and explicit missing values

Candidate source tokens available after STEP-02:

- `resource.url`
- `resource.host`
- `resource.path`
- `resource.filename`
- `resource.basename`
- `resource.extension`
- `resource.mime`
- `resource.kind`
- `resource.chrome_type`
- `resource.status`
- `resource.content_length`
- `resource.initiator`
- `resource.observed_at`
- `watch.started_at`

Reserve, but do not yet define STEP-03 semantics for, `page.url`, `page.title`, `page.origin`, and `field.<slug>`.

Do not expose cookies, Authorization-class headers, arbitrary raw request/response headers, request/response bodies, arbitrary raw JSON, environment variables, or arbitrary plugin properties as baseline tokens.

Template candidate syntax is `${resource.url}` with optional safe left-to-right transforms, for example `${resource.basename|default:"resource"|filename_safe}`. Baseline safe transforms: `default`, `trim`, `lower`, `upper`, `slice`, literal `replace`, `urlencode`, and `filename_safe`. Unknown token/transform/syntax is a validation error before save/Watch.

Missing is a distinct sentinel, not an empty string. A required unresolved token fails that action template unless an explicit `default` transform handles it. This intentionally rejects JDownloader's current missing-plugin-property -> empty-string behavior.

For a matched rule, action application is atomic: if any action template cannot be evaluated, apply none of that rule's actions, record a bounded evaluation error, and stop further rules for that candidate. This avoids partially mutated derived state.

### 7. New-resource semantics and preview parity

Packagizer evaluation occurs once when a resource candidate is first admitted with sufficient response/classification facts. Later completion can enrich immutable observation metadata but does not silently re-run historical Packagizer decisions. Rule edits do not retroactively mutate existing resources. A future explicit re-evaluate command may be researched separately; it is not baseline authority.

The Test/Preview surface must invoke the same evaluator contract as live admission. It is a dry run: no network effects, no mutation of observation history, and no external transmission. It should explain:

- classification evidence and chosen semantic kind;
- selected type/include/exclude decisions and veto reason;
- rule order and enabled/skipped state;
- each condition result;
- token resolution state including `missing`;
- safe transform/application result;
- derived-field before/after assignment;
- stop-processing or evaluation-error reason.

Persisted diagnostics/explanations should store bounded structural facts (rule IDs, condition/action IDs, boolean outcomes, error codes, classification reason codes) without copying raw URLs/token values by default. An interactive local preview may display the selected local resource's current values to the user, but that does not authorize durable diagnostic retention or telemetry.

## Explicit retain/adopt/adapt/defer/reject summary

- **retain:** explicit Watch boundary, local-first/payload-minimized capture, immutable history.
- **adopt:** ordered rule list, stop-processing, Test/Preview, newly-observed semantics, compact template vocabulary.
- **adapt:** Cat Catch/JDownloader template UX into namespaced crsniffer tokens with safe transforms and explicit missing state; wildcard filtering into dedicated resource-glob grammar.
- **defer:** regex conditions/transforms, explicit historical re-evaluation, import/export UX/file format, rich package/download actions, page/field token evaluation until STEP-03.
- **reject:** exact `<jd:...>` syntax, environment/plugin-property namespaces, cookies/raw payload/credential tokens, hidden source mutation between ordered rules, missing->empty coercion, arbitrary JS/eval.

## Open items handed forward

STEP-03 owns page-aware XPath fields and `page.*`/`field.*` token population/invalidation. STEP-04 owns persistence technology, retention, UI/editor design, list virtualization, exact filename/path platform sanitization policy and storage of saved definitions. STEP-05 owns final ADR acceptance and versioned public contract decisions.
</file>

