# v0.0.3 STEP-03 page-mapping and document-context authority capture

## Scope

Research-only capture for ROADMAP-0001 STEP-03. Source facts, read-only donor observations, and crsniffer interpretations are deliberately separated. No extension runtime/browser probe or donor execution was performed.

## Primary source facts

### Chrome webNavigation: document identity and navigation lifecycle

Source: Chrome Extensions `chrome.webNavigation`, retrieved 2026-08-19.
https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/reference/api/webNavigation

- Successful navigation order is `onBeforeNavigate -> onCommitted -> [onDOMContentLoaded] -> onCompleted`.
- History API changes emit `onHistoryStateUpdated`; fragment changes emit `onReferenceFragmentUpdated`; both can happen after DOMContentLoaded/Completed.
- BFCache restoration may not emit DOMContentLoaded.
- Chrome defines no ordering between `webRequest` and `webNavigation`; request events can still arrive for old frames around a new navigation.
- `frameId` can remain stable across document navigations while `documentId` changes for a new document.
- `documentId` remains stable across a document's prerender/active/cached lifecycle changes.
- Main frame has frameId 0; child frames carry parent document/frame evidence.

### Chrome scripting: exact-document execution

Source: Chrome Extensions `chrome.scripting`, retrieved 2026-08-19.
https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/reference/api/scripting

- `scripting` requires extension permission plus host authority/activeTab as applicable.
- InjectionTarget supports `documentIds` (Chrome 106+) for exact-document targeting; documentIds and frameIds are mutually exclusive target modes.
- Execution defaults to the ISOLATED world.
- Injection results carry documentId/frameId, allowing identity validation before a result is consumed.

### Chrome runtime/message sender and webRequest document evidence

Sources, retrieved 2026-08-19:
https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/reference/api/runtime
https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/reference/api/webRequest

- Runtime MessageSender can expose documentId, documentLifecycle, frameId, origin, URL and tab; lifecycle reflects connection-creation state and can subsequently change.
- webRequest details can expose documentId/parentDocumentId/frameId/frameType/initiator/requestId depending on event/context.
- These surfaces provide document evidence but do not remove the documented webRequest/webNavigation ordering race.

### DOM selection and attribute absence

Source: WHATWG DOM Standard, retrieved 2026-08-19.
https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/

- `querySelectorAll()` returns a static selector result.
- `getAttribute()` returns null when an attribute is absent, which is semantically distinct from an existing empty attribute.

### XPath result types and mutation behavior

Source: W3C DOM Level 3 XPath, retrieved 2026-08-19.
https://www.w3.org/TR/DOM-Level-3-XPath/

- XPath evaluation is relative to a context node and can return number, string, boolean or node-set result types.
- Ordered node iterators are invalidated by document mutation.
- Ordered node snapshots remain usable after mutation, although reevaluating the XPath may produce different results and captured nodes themselves can have changed/moved/been removed.

### HTML rendered text and markup serialization

Sources: WHATWG HTML Standard, retrieved 2026-08-19.
https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dom.html#the-innertext-idl-attribute
https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dynamic-markup-insertion.html

- `innerText` exposes rendered-text semantics, not simply raw descendant text.
- `innerHTML`/`outerHTML` serialize markup, which is a larger/more sensitive data surface than text/attribute/scalar values.

## Read-only crscraper v0.0.37 donor observations

Immutable donor: `dumps/donors/crscraper/crscraper.v0.0.37.zip`
ZIP SHA-256: `6fc4ee4997c1901489f75d37bac3d976067ac1208faee04f99bb46a581507e8a`
Promoted tree SHA-256: `ee41d9e0a3f2ab31d81c224470696ce5fc748f1b3f8d9c15ded2c61638f13b4b`

Bounded archive inspection found mature precedent for CSS/XPath sharing a selector envelope while retaining native result differences; normalizing XPath node sets to ordered snapshots; treating Missing as an extraction state; explicit read/cardinality/fallback stages; ordered full-candidate mappings; exact committed documentId stale-result authority; and Preview/diagnostics using the same semantic stages as live behavior.

This donor material is evidence only. It was not executed/imported/built/tested/benchmarked/probed as crsniffer.

## crsniffer interpretation

1. `PageContext` represents the watched top-level document, not blindly the document that initiated a particular request.
2. ResourceObservation retains enough captured attribution to say which top-level document, if any, it belongs to. Ambiguous attribution fails closed for page fields.
3. `documentId` is the hard stale boundary. Same-document History/fragment navigation uses an additional `pageRevision` invalidation generation because URL/DOM meaning can change without a new document.
4. Page fields evaluate on demand against an exact active target document; baseline does not promise continuous DOM freshness via a hidden always-on observer.
5. CSS node results and XPath node results normalize to ordered snapshot-like values. XPath string/number/boolean results remain typed scalars.
6. Baseline reads stay bounded to text/rendered text/attribute/direct primitive property/raw XPath scalar. `innerHTML`/`outerHTML` are deferred.
7. Missing, null, empty string, false and zero remain separate. Candidate fallback advances on Missing or explicit rejection, not on structural/evaluation errors.
8. Named PageFieldMapping definitions are reusable. Watch freezes only mappings referenced by its frozen enabled rules/templates.
9. `page.*` and `field.*` are immutable source facts for Packagizer evaluation. Stale/unattributed context yields Missing, never values from the current replacement page.
10. Preview and live evaluation share the same mapping/Packagizer semantic engines and structural diagnostics.

## Proposed STEP-03 contract candidates

- PageContext identity: `WatchSessionId + tabId + topDocumentId + pageRevision`.
- Page built-ins: `${page.url}`, `${page.origin}`, `${page.title}`.
- Named field token: `${field.<slug>}`, slug grammar `^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,63}$`.
- Selector families: CSS + XPath.
- Node reads: `text_content`, `inner_text`, `attribute(name)`, direct primitive `property(name)`.
- XPath scalar read: `raw`.
- Cardinality: `first`, `optional_one`, `exact_one`, `many`.
- Safe page transforms: shared typed registry subset `trim`, `lower`, `upper`, `slice`, literal `replace`, `to_string`, `join`.
- Markup reads, arbitrary JS/eval, dotted arbitrary property traversal, baseline subframe mapping editor, and continuous MutationObserver mapping are deferred/rejected as documented in matrices.

## Unresolved for later steps

- STEP-04: actual runtime/framework implementation mechanism, storage model, caching/performance policy, mapping editor layout, accessibility and Side Panel information architecture.
- STEP-05: final public serialization/ADR acceptance and whether any deferred page-read/transform capability is required by evidence.
</file>

