<file path="docs/reference/resources.md">
# Resource classification and admission authority

`v0.0.12 / ROADMAP-0002 STEP-07` adds a pure derived-resource layer over immutable `ResourceObservation` records. It does not change the IndexedDB physical schema and does not introduce Packagizer rule execution.

## Canonical URL

- Parse with the platform `URL` implementation.
- Accept HTTP(S) only.
- Serialize using normal URL serialization and remove only the fragment.
- Preserve query serialization and order; do not sort or drop query parameters.
- Derive a bounded lowercase path extension only from the final path segment.

## Classification

Kinds are `image`, `video`, `stream_manifest`, `audio`, `other`, and `unknown`.

Evidence priority is deliberately bounded:

1. recognized non-generic response MIME type;
2. Chrome `image` request type as an image fallback;
3. allowlisted path extension;
4. otherwise `unknown` (including generic Chrome `media` without stronger evidence).

HLS is recognized from `application/vnd.apple.mpegurl`, `audio/mpegurl`, `.m3u8`, or `.m3u`. DASH is recognized from `application/dash+xml` or `.mpd`. A recognized MIME result wins over a conflicting extension, while the conflict remains visible in classification evidence.

Primary current authorities checked for this iteration:

- WHATWG URL Standard, URL serialization: https://url.spec.whatwg.org/
- RFC 8216 HTTP Live Streaming, playlist identification: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8216.html
- IANA Media Types registry, `application/dash+xml`: https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types/media-types.xhtml

## Admission filter v1

The v1 filter is frozen into the WatchSession as a `resourceFilter` definition body:

- `images: boolean`
- `videos: boolean`
- `includeGlobs: string[]`
- `excludeGlobs: string[]`

Default policy enables Images and Videos. Videos includes both `video` and recognized `stream_manifest`; audio is not selected by that preset.

Admission is exactly:

`(selected preset OR matching include glob) AND NOT matching exclude glob`

If there is no positive lane, the resource is not admitted. Exclude is always the final veto.

Glob v1 matches the entire canonical URL, is case-sensitive, supports only `*` and `?`, and permits explicit escapes only for `*`, `?`, and `\\`. Regex is not part of v1.

## Reversible dedup projection

Raw observations are never deleted or merged. UI-facing groups use:

`WatchSession + document identity + canonical URL`

Each group retains every occurrence and original observation ID, plus total and admitted occurrence counts. Query differences remain distinct because the canonical URL preserves the query.

## Watch-start validation

An invalid frozen resource filter or glob aborts Watch start before lifecycle/session authority becomes active. The composed WatchSession is rolled back by the existing start transaction boundary.
</file>

<file path="docs/reference/side-panel-control-plane.md">
# Side Panel Control Plane

`crsniffer` v0.0.21 keeps the Side Panel as a reconstructable client over background/durable authority. Control-plane schema remains v2. Presentation state, service-worker globals, row buffers and runtime Ports are never canonical product state.

## Correctness authority

One-shot request/response operations are the correctness path. Each lane carries a positive client `requestSequence`; the panel applies a response only when the echoed sequence correlates and no newer request owns that lane. Slow/out-of-order responses cannot overwrite newer presentation state.

`panel.hydrate` remains bounded to current control context:

- selected tab ID plus URL/title/incognito only when Chrome exposes those properties under existing authority;
- Watch/readiness state;
- frozen active Watch setup/filter context loaded from immutable WatchSession authority;
- original active-Watch tab context;
- advisory recent runtime health (`healthy` or redacted storage/evaluation/capture degradation).

STEP-03 adds three separate bounded one-shot query lanes:

- `panel.resources.sessions` — recent human Watch-session summaries;
- `panel.resources.page` — one bounded newest/older resource page under one WatchSession and current search/kind/status filters;
- `panel.resources.detail` — one selected occurrence detail.

Hydration, session-list, resource-page and resource-detail each have independent freshness sequencing so a slower response in one lane cannot suppress or replace newer state in another lane.

## Durable query boundary

The background resource query service reads existing IndexedDB v1 authority only. Recent sessions use `watchSessions.byCreatedAt`; resource pages use `resourceObservations.byWatchSessionObservedAt` with stable timestamp + primary-key continuation. No new store/index/materialized UI projection is introduced.

The Side Panel uses a shallow bounded row buffer and pure virtual-window calculation. Append invalidation means “query the newest bounded page and reconcile by stable observation identity”; it is not an append-state payload from the Port.

## Runtime Port role

The runtime Port is `crsniffer.panel.v2`. Schema-v2 messages remain invalidation hints only: `connected`, `watch_changed`, `observation_persisted`, `configuration_changed`, `runtime_degraded`, or `runtime_recovered`. A hint means “re-query/reconcile authority”; it is never a state snapshot.

Malformed hints are ignored. Disconnect/worker termination cannot change durable Watch/resource state. The panel reconnects and re-queries bounded authority.

## Resource presentation boundary

The primary results list shows admitted projected/failed occurrences and keeps each occurrence separately identifiable. Rejected observations remain outside the normal result list. Human session selection uses setup name/start time/live context rather than requiring opaque WatchSession IDs.

Selected detail exposes accepted source/response facts, derived Packagizer summary, page-enrichment status and structural explanation. Raw headers/body/cookies/credentials, raw request/document identifiers and internal rule IDs are not part of the normal detail contract.

## STEP-03 boundary

No Rules/Templates editor, Token Browser, Preview/Test, Page Field editor/assist, Settings workflow, new Chrome permission, IndexedDB schema/store change or streaming canonical Port cache is introduced. v0.0.30 STEP-12 measured the current page/scan/buffer architecture and retained the constants because no tuning was justified. Port invalidations are now coalesced into a 50ms presentation refresh wave; one-shot queries remain correctness authority. These constants are regression-tested implementation bounds, not product maxima.
</file>

