<file path="docs/reference/ui-interaction-authority.md">
# Side Panel UI Interaction Authority

`crsniffer` v0.0.19 freezes the normal interaction model before broad Watch/Rules/Settings implementation. The normative record is `ADR-0006`; the complete task/owner inventory is `MATRIX-0016`.

## Working copies and saved definitions

Side Panel forms may hold disposable **working copies**. Selecting a saved Watch setup, resource filter, Rule, Template or Page Field deep-copies its values plus source revision provenance into the editor. Selection never starts Watch or mutates the saved item.

- **New** creates an unsaved working copy.
- **Save as** creates a new durable saved definition.
- **Update** writes only with the expected saved revision.
- **Reset** discards local edits and reloads the saved source after confirmation.
- **Duplicate** creates a new unsaved copy.
- **Delete** removes only the exact loaded revision when it is not protected and not referenced by another current saved definition. Immutable WatchSession snapshots are never rewritten or treated as live references.
- Revision conflict preserves the user's work and fails closed; reload or save-as-new is explicit.

A working copy is presentation state, not a new durable store. Watch execution still composes an immutable WatchSession only from a persisted Watch-definition revision (or the built-in Default Watch). Dirty/new Watch-affecting edits must be explicitly published before Start uses them.

## Human vocabulary

Normal UI uses task language: **Current page**, **Site access**, **Images**, **Videos**, **Include URL patterns**, **Exclude URL patterns**, **Resource**, **Occurrences**, **Watch setup**, **Rule**, **Template**, **Token**, **Page Field**, **History retention**, **Clear data**, and **Diagnostics**. Raw IDs, revisions, digests, document IDs and structural traces are advanced/support detail.

## Pickers and ordering

Native `select` is the default saved-item picker. A searchable custom combobox is justified only by real scale/task evidence and must carry complete keyboard/focus/name/value/popup behavior.

Every reorderable list provides Move up/down or equivalent non-drag single-pointer controls that are also keyboard operable. Drag may be convenience only.

## Progressive disclosure and shared states

Critical page/scope, readiness, Site access, validation, stale/conflict and destructive-scope information stays in the normal task flow. Uncommon filters/transforms, raw IDs/schema and structural traces may be disclosed as Advanced/Support detail.

Shared saved-editor state language is **New**, **Loaded**, and **Modified** with explicit `Loaded from … · revision N` provenance. Operational state language remains **No items yet**, **Loading…**, **Watching**, **Reconnecting…**, **Page changed**, **Saved item changed elsewhere**, **Limited operation — reason**, and action-specific errors.

Passive status never steals focus. Live resource updates are summarized rather than announced row by row.

## Narrow Side Panel accessibility

The narrow one-column task/DOM order is canonical. Normal content targets 320 CSS px-equivalent reflow without page-level two-dimensional scrolling. Native controls are preferred. Frequent/custom targets follow WCAG 2.2 AA target-size/spacing expectations. Watch / Rules / Settings uses APG-compatible tab semantics, with manual activation as the baseline. Focus is restored after transient editors/dialogs when the invoker still exists, non-essential motion respects `prefers-reduced-motion`, and user-facing strings must be externalizable through Chrome i18n when localization is implemented.

## Page Field assistance

Future Page Field Test/sample/highlight/pick is bounded and user initiated. It must reuse existing optional Site access plus exact-document isolated-world scripting, revalidate page/document revision, discard stale results, and keep CSS/XPath directly editable. It does not authorize `activeTab`, MAIN-world injection, persistent page observers, debugger/CDP, broad host access, or arbitrary user JavaScript.

## Implemented Watch/resource state

`v0.0.20 / ROADMAP-0003 STEP-02` implements this grammar for Watch: native saved-setup loading, New, Save as, revision-fenced Update, Reset, Images/Videos, Advanced include/exclude URL patterns, explicit Site access, exact-revision Start, frozen active-session context and explicit termination/degradation copy. A dirty/new/stale/problem working copy cannot start Watch.

`v0.0.21 / STEP-03` adds bounded recent-session/resource paging, stable virtualized occurrence rows, URL/filename/group search, kind/status filters, summarized live additions and selected-resource detail. Session selection is human-facing, same-URL occurrences remain distinct, structural explanation is progressive and raw sensitive/request/document/internal-rule payloads remain outside normal inspection. Unsupported arbitrary grouping/sort is not fabricated merely to satisfy a UI checklist.

## Implemented reusable-definition lifecycle

`v0.0.25 / ROADMAP-0003 STEP-07` completes the shared saved-definition grammar across Watch, Rule, Template and Page Field. Reverse-reference deletion checks live in the definition layer; Default Watch is protected; custom Watch cleanup can remove only its exact orphaned internal filter; successful deletes use ordinary invalidation hints. Raw identity/revision/schema-shaped draft inspection is available only under collapsed **Advanced technical details** and is not a Load/Set/Import/Export format. Native saved-item selectors remain the default because no current Crsniffer library scale justifies a custom searchable combobox.

## Next implementation boundary

`v0.0.26 / ROADMAP-0003 STEP-08` established typed Settings/Clear/privacy/diagnostics/support/preferences. `v0.0.27 / STEP-09` closed whole-product shell/accessibility/localization. `v0.0.28 / STEP-10` adds bounded terminal-history retention, approximate pressure and restart recovery without changing the interaction authority. STEP-11+ packaged-host security/capacity/release work remains later.
</file>

<file path="docs/reference/watch-runtime.md">
# Watch Runtime Authority

Current baseline: `v0.0.21` / ROADMAP-0003 STEP-03.

## Permission boundary

- Required runtime API permissions remain `storage`, `webRequest`, `webNavigation`, and `scripting`.
- Runtime optional host authority remains `http://*/*` and `https://*/*`.
- The Side Panel exposes an explicit **Grant site access** user action; complete HTTP+HTTPS coverage is rechecked before background Start.
- Crsniffer does not add `tabs` or `activeTab`; exact URL/title projection is shown when existing matching host authority makes those sensitive tab properties available.
- Incognito and non-HTTP(S) tabs are rejected before WatchSession composition.

## Lifecycle ownership

- Exactly one Watch may be active.
- WatchSession remains an immutable exact-revision frozen definition snapshot.
- IndexedDB `meta` keeps mutable lifecycle history; `chrome.storage.session` keeps browser-lifetime active coordination.
- Service-worker restart may reconcile the same active Watch while session coordination survives.
- Browser/extension runtime restart without session coordination never auto-resumes a historical Watch and records `runtime_session_lost`.
- Stop, target-tab close and permission loss terminate active Watch deterministically.

## Watch setup publication

The panel may hold a disposable Watch/filter working copy. `watch.configurations` returns bounded saved setup summaries. `watch.configuration.save` provides explicit Save as / exact-revision Update. Start accepts the exact saved Watch definition/revision; dirty/new/stale/problem local state cannot become runtime authority.

The built-in Default Watch remains a valid first-run saved authority. Active Watch context is reconstructed from immutable WatchSession definitions, so later saved-definition edits never alter the running session.

## Resource history projection

`v0.0.21` adds read-only bounded history over existing immutable authority:

- recent Watch sessions are listed from the existing `watchSessions.byCreatedAt` index;
- resource pages are read from `resourceObservations.byWatchSessionObservedAt` with a stable timestamp + primary-key continuation cursor;
- older continuation uses index cursor positioning rather than materializing the full history;
- rejected observations remain outside the primary admitted-result list;
- each admitted occurrence remains a separate row even when multiple rows share the same canonical URL;
- selected detail is reconstructed from persisted source/evaluation facts and excludes raw sensitive payloads plus request/document/internal-rule identifiers from the normal contract.

The current 40-row default page, 100-row maximum page, 500-row bounded scan and 500-row Side Panel buffer are implementation constants, not final capacity-certified limits.

## Runtime operations

Current Watch operations include:

- `watch.status`
- `watch.start`
- `watch.stop`
- `watch.configurations`
- `watch.configuration.save`

Current bounded control-plane query operations include:

- `panel.hydrate`
- `panel.resources.sessions`
- `panel.resources.page`
- `panel.resources.detail`

The three resource lanes have independent request freshness sequences. Runtime Ports remain invalidation hints only and never carry canonical resource state.

## Degraded operation

Recent background capture failures are projected as redacted advisory storage/evaluation/capture health. This health signal does not mutate Watch authority. A later successful persisted observation recovers advisory health.

## Next boundary

`v0.0.24 / ROADMAP-0003 STEP-06` adds structured Page Field authoring plus exact-document Test/Highlight/Pick over the existing optional-host + isolated scripting boundary. Assistance never creates durable authoring state and stale document/revision results fail closed. `v0.0.25 / STEP-07` unified reusable lifecycle is next; Settings and new permissions remain later work.
</file>

<file path="docs/release/store-readiness.md">
# v0.0.31 store-readiness and package boundary

## Product purpose

Crsniffer is a local Chrome Side Panel tool for an explicit user-started Watch that observes permitted page network resources, applies deterministic local filters/rules/Page Fields, and presents local history. It is not a general browser monitor, downloader, telemetry client, or remote scraping service.

## Final repository-owned manifest intent

Required extension API permissions remain:

- `storage` — local durable/preferences/session coordination;
- `webRequest` — passive resource observation during an active Watch;
- `webNavigation` — exact document/navigation attribution;
- `scripting` — explicit exact-document Page Field Test/Highlight/Pick.

HTTP/HTTPS host access remains `optional_host_permissions` and user-mediated. Incognito is disabled. Extension pages use self-only CSP. No `activeTab`, broad `tabs`, debugger, downloads, native messaging, offscreen document, unlimited storage, clipboard permission, external messaging surface, content-script baseline, web-accessible resource surface, MAIN-world scripting, remote executable code, or telemetry permission is introduced by STEP-13.

## Data and privacy disclosure

Crsniffer stores accepted Watch configuration and local resource/history data under the extension origin. Raw headers, cookies, bodies, credentials, request IDs and document IDs are not part of ordinary durable observation history. URL userinfo is stripped and non-HTTP(S) payload-bearing URLs are reduced to scheme-only representation before persistence. Clear/retention controls are exposed in Settings. Chrome-granted Site access remains a separate permission surface and is never silently revoked by local-data clearing.

No product telemetry/upload authority is present.

## Native-aware appearance

The Side Panel follows public browser/web-platform appearance rather than hard-coded branding colors: system font stack, `color-scheme: light dark`, CSS system colors, native control accent behavior, forced-color support and no fixed hex/RGB/HSL palette. See `docs/reference/native-chrome-theme.md`.

Exact arbitrary Chrome custom-theme hue matching is not claimed because Chrome does not expose its private browser-chrome/WebUI color tokens as a supported extension-page contract.

## Build/package environment disposition

Repository source authority is complete and statically checkable, but the current environment does not contain the selected package installation:

- package manager declared by source: `npm@11.17.0`;
- installed npm in this container: not the selected package-manager version;
- `package-lock.json`: unavailable;
- `node_modules`: unavailable;
- selected WXT/vue-tsc executables and generated `.wxt/tsconfig.json`: unavailable.

Under `agents/governance/environment-deferrals.md`, these are **`deferred_environment` and non-blocking**. STEP-13 does not claim native WXT build/package/install/update or Chrome Web Store submission passed here.

Repository substitute evidence is executed instead:

- exact dependency versions are pinned in `package.json`;
- manifest/CSP/permission/i18n/package-shape authority is source-tested;
- persistence logical v1 -> v2 and legacy Settings v1 -> v2 upgrade behavior is repository-tested;
- deterministic recovery/source package is sealed and cold-verified by `workspace-manager.py`;
- `scripts/check-release-readiness.mjs` emits machine-readable repository pass + environment deferral status.

## Capable-environment follow-up

When the selected package environment is available, resolve the existing deferral in place by:

1. producing and reviewing the reproducible npm lock with the declared package-manager/tool versions;
2. hydrating only from that lock;
3. running selected `vue-tsc`, WXT build and WXT package;
4. hashing and independently rebuilding the generated extension package;
5. loading the generated unpacked extension in Chrome and exercising install/update from the prior promoted persisted state;
6. inspecting the generated manifest/CSP/permissions/locales and the light/dark Side Panel in the packaged extension origin;
7. retaining the evidence as resolution of the environment deferral, not rewriting v0.0.31 as though those checks ran here.

## Store publication status

Chrome Web Store account/submission/review evidence is `deferred_environment`. v0.0.31 is store-readiness certification of repository-owned intent and recovery identity, not a claim that a public listing has been submitted or approved.
</file>

